# The worker's collector and JIT shape, baked into the launcher rather than left to each overlay's
# JAVA_OPTS: the image's AOT cache (tools.ClassArchiveTraining) is trained through the same
# launcher, and a cache trained under another collector or type-speculation setting does not map,
# silently putting every class back into metaspace. AotCacheOptionsSpec holds the overlays to these.
# SerialGC and no type speculation were carried from Fly as the fix for an OOM-restart loop under G1.
-XX:+UseSerialGC
-XX:-UseTypeSpeculation
# Map the AOT cache at its own base instead of a random one: relocating it patches every pointer and
# dirties every page, so the whole archive became private memory (worker image, PL options: 741 MB
# anonymous RSS relocated, 638 MB in place). Diagnostic: jdk25-parity.options unlocks it first.
-XX:ArchiveRelocationMode=0
# Cap the temporary direct buffers NIO caches per thread: a socket read into a heap buffer goes
# through a direct one of the same size, kept per thread and unbounded by default. NMT's "Other" held
# 161 MB of them on worker-uk (~75 buffers of ~2 MB, the Mongo driver's replies) until this cap.
-Djdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize=262144
# Share the byte arrays of equal Strings the collector keeps: 57.9 MB of worker-pl's heap was
# duplicate contents ("US", "2D", titles, JSON values); 11 MB off the live old generation measured.
-XX:+UseStringDeduplication
# Cache the boxes of small Integers up to 16,384, not 127: the identity model keeps its family ids —
# recycled, so below the live family count (~2,200 on US) — in five maps and sets, and every one was
# a fresh Integer (~10 MB on worker-us). The cache itself is ~320 KB.
-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax=16384
# Let the code cache grow by half its size, not 15%, before the collector is asked to unload cold code:
# at 15% of the 64m cache every ~10 MB of new code forced a SerialOld FULL collection — 7 in a boot,
# then one every ~90 s. Boots with nothing to re-resolve (2026-10-03, pl/us): pl ~25% less CPU over a
# boot's first 5 and 10 minutes at the same 64m cache, us 5-7%, the JIT and GC each ~15% less on us.
# Re-measured on JDK 27 (2026-10-23), where the sweeper is gone but this still sets when code-cache
# growth asks for a collection: one fixture boot under PL's options ran 6 "CodeCache GC Threshold"
# collections and 9 full GCs at 27's default 15, 1 and 5 at 50 (full-GC pause time halved).
-XX:SweeperThreshold=50
# Compile a method only after three times the default invocations: the JIT was half of a boot's CPU, much
# of it on code a boot runs a few thousand times and never again. At 2 (2026-10-03), us boots: JIT -18% and
# peak 30 s ~1.25 cores (was 1.4-2.1); at 3, us JIT at 5 min -16% more (77-95 -> 68-84 s); neither cost
# CPU 10-30 minutes after boot. 4 measured no different from 3 on us/pl/uk boots, so 3 it stays.
-XX:CompileThresholdScaling=3
# Page the whole heap in at boot rather than on first use. -Xms equals -Xmx, so the heap is committed
# from the start but its pages reach RSS only when first written: worker-pl's RSS stepped +120 MB
# 13-23 minutes into a boot (2026-10-07 22:06, 10-12 01:22 -- NMT's committed total unchanged, the
# off-book gap -45 -> +76 MB), and that bounded step read as a climb on JvmResidentNearContainerLimit's
# 30-minute predict_linear. Touching ~0.5-1.2 GB costs well under a second of a boot.
-XX:+AlwaysPreTouch
